data.content:
Use EF Core 7+'s **`ExecuteUpdateAsync`** and **`ExecuteDeleteAsync`** — they run a single SQL statement server-side without loading entities into memory:

```csharp
// Operation 1: Bulk update — single UPDATE statement
var cutoff = DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-6);
await _db.Products
    .Where(p => p.LastSoldDate < cutoff)
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.IsActive, false));

// Operation 2: Bulk delete — single DELETE statement
var logCutoff = DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1);
await _db.AuditLogs
    .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < logCutoff)
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
```

**Why this is fast:**
- Generates a single `UPDATE`/`DELETE` SQL statement
- No entities loaded into memory (no tracking overhead)
- No `SaveChangesAsync()` needed — executes immediately

**Caveats:**
- Bypasses change tracker — any already-loaded entities won't reflect the change
- Bypasses `SaveChanges` interceptors/events — if you rely on those, handle separately
- For the 2M delete, consider batching if your DB has lock escalation concerns:

```csharp
int deleted;
do
{
    deleted = await _db.AuditLogs
        .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < logCutoff)
        .Take(10_000)
        .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
} while (deleted > 0);
```
